From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: give the charger its interrupt

Unplug the cable at 100% and the phone does not notice. UPower keeps
reporting the charger online -- observed still saying "online: yes" ten
minutes after the cable came out -- so UPower.OnBattery stays false,
PowerDevil keeps the plugged-in policy, and a locked screen never leads to
a suspend.

The charger node has no interrupt. bq256xx registers
bq256xx_irq_handler_thread() only `if (client->irq)`, and that handler is
the only thing in the driver that calls power_supply_changed() on a state
change. With no interrupt there is no uevent, and nothing ever tells
userspace the cable moved. The values in sysfs are correct the moment
anything reads them, which is what makes this hard to see: the kernel says
online=0 and status=Discharging while UPower, which is event driven, still
believes what it read at startup.

	# unplugged ten minutes earlier
	upower: online: yes   (updated 574 seconds ago)
	kernel: bq256xx-charger/online = 0
	busctl ... UPower OnBattery -> b false

	# udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=power_supply
	upower: online: no
	busctl ... UPower OnBattery -> b true

The comment this replaces called the interrupt optional, which is what the
binding says and is true of the driver in the narrow sense -- it reads
registers on demand and charging works without it. It is not true of the
system: without it nothing is ever pushed, and every consumer that waits to
be told sees a stale world.

The number is the vendor's, from discrete_charging_rhodep.dtsi, and tlmm 12
is unclaimed in this port. The driver requests IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING, which
is what the type here says.

This is the second half of "the phone will not suspend at 100%". The first
was patch 0096, where the fuel gauge reported FULL while discharging; with
that fixed the battery correctly reads Discharging at 100% but the daemon
still said OnBattery: false, because the daemon asks the charger, not the
gauge.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -596,14 +596,28 @@
 	 * which is what we want: if it expires the part resets its registers to
 	 * the factory values and charging stops.
 	 *
-	 * The interrupt (tlmm 12 in the vendor DT) is left out on purpose: it is
-	 * optional in bq256xx and without it the driver reads the registers on
-	 * demand. It can be added later.
+	 * The interrupt is not optional in practice, whatever the binding says.
+	 * Without it bq256xx_irq_handler_thread() never runs, so nothing ever
+	 * calls power_supply_changed() for this supply and userspace is never
+	 * told the cable moved. UPower keeps whatever it read at startup --
+	 * observed holding "online: yes" for ten minutes after the charger was
+	 * unplugged -- so UPower.OnBattery stays false, and PowerDevil applies
+	 * the plugged-in policy: a locked screen never leads to a suspend. A
+	 * udevadm trigger on the power_supply subsystem fixes it immediately,
+	 * which is what identified this.
+	 *
+	 * tlmm 12, from the vendor's discrete_charging_rhodep.dtsi:
+	 *	interrupt-parent = <&tlmm>;
+	 *	interrupts = <12 0>;
+	 *	sgm,irq-gpio = <&tlmm 12 0>;
+	 * The driver asks for IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING, and the part pulls the line
+	 * low on a state change, so the type here matches.
 	 */
 	charger: charger@3b {
 		compatible = "sgm,sgm41542";
 		#cooling-cells = <2>;
 		reg = <0x3b>;
+		interrupts-extended = <&tlmm 12 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING>;
 		monitored-battery = <&battery>;
 		input-current-limit-microamp = <1000000>;
 	};
